UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 22, Lesson 2: Creating an advertisement', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo space) and ethical limits—no fake scarcity, no body-shaming, no targeting harmful stereotypes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a concept brief: audience, offer, hero image, one ethical limit.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Coherent brief; limit concrete.\n\nCHECK\nCopying a real brand’s logo/slogan. Create original school-safe ads.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign an original print or slide ad with brief and a three-sentence ethics check.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo space) and ethical limits—no fake scarcity, no body-shaming, no targeting harmful stereotypes.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design an original print or slide ad with brief and a three-sentence ethics check. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using ad concept on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan.. Correction: Strong answer: Coherent brief;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan. Create original school-safe ads. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating ad concept on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with ad concept, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about ad concept with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for ad concept. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for ad concept. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Coherent brief; limit concrete"],"question":"Write a concept brief: audience, offer, hero image, one ethical limit. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for ad concept; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Coherent brief; limit concrete","Careful use of target and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan","Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with ad concept?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named target and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan","Name target and explain why it matters only, without linking it to ad concept","Apply ad concept to new material and name the target and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies ad concept with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries ad concept. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show ad concept and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan"],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses ad concept from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Coherent brief; limit concrete","Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name target and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about ad concept?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse target and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for ad concept?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about ad concept","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of ad concept on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about ad concept with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating ad concept on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using ad concept on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for ad concept. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with ad concept, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan.. Correction: Strong answer: Coherent brief;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w22-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise ad concept.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use ad concept with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify target and explain why it matters.","I can practise visual plan independently on a new prompt.","I can notice ethical limit needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo space) and ethical limits—no fake scarcity, no body-shaming, no targeting harmful stereotypes.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ad concept with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nProduct: reusable bottle club at school. Target: Year 7. Hero: student filling bottle at bubbler. Limit: no claiming it cures health issues.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a concept brief: audience, offer, hero image, one ethical limit.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Coherent brief; limit concrete.","explain":"Creating an advertisement starts with target audience, a single clear offer, a visual plan (hero image, colour, logo space) and ethical limits—no fake scarcity, no body-shaming, no targeting harmful stereotypes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Copying a real brand’s logo/slogan. Create original school-safe ads. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Ad concept.","caption":"Ethics limits.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Audience</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">who</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Offer</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one clear ask</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Product: reusable bottle club at school. Target: Year 7. Hero: student filling bottle at bubbler. Limit: no claiming it  Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Product: reusable bottle club at school. Target: Year 7. Hero: student filling bottle at bubbler. Limit: no claiming it  Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ad concept with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nProduct: reusable bottle club at school. Target: Year 7. Hero: student filling bottle at bubbler. Limit: no claiming it cures health issues. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision 